        <div id="heat-the-person" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Heat the Person, Not the Room: Why a Heated Blanket Cuts Heating Bills
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best heated blanket for most homes is a certified electric blanket or heated mattress pad with an automatic shut-off, used so you can turn the thermostat down. That last part is the point. A furnace or a space heater warms every cubic foot of air in a room, the walls, the furniture and the gap under the door, so that a little of that warmth reaches you. A heated blanket skips all of that and puts its heat against your body, under a layer that holds it in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most people buy one because they are cold, and then keep the heating exactly where it was. That gets you comfort, but not savings. The savings come from letting the house run cooler at night and in the rooms where you sit still, and using the blanket to make that bearable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Watts Gap</h3>

          <p>
            A plug-in space heater on high draws about 1,500 watts, the usual ceiling for a plug-in heater. A household electric blanket, throw or mattress pad is rated for a small fraction of that; the exact figure is printed on the rating label on the controller or tag, and it varies by size and model. Even before you count the furnace, swapping an evening of space heater for an evening of heated throw is a large cut in the electricity used to keep one person warm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Turn the Thermostat Down, Not Off</h3>

          <p>
            The useful move is a few degrees lower at night and in the evening, with a heated blanket in the bed and a heated throw on the sofa. Do not let the house get cold enough to freeze pipes or grow condensation and mould on cold walls; a heated blanket warms people, not plumbing. If one room is always cold, our <a href="/best-electric-space-heater/">electric space heater guide</a> covers how to heat that one room efficiently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Heated Blanket Does Not Help</h3>

          <p>
            It will not dry out damp air, warm a cold bathroom, or keep a toddler warm, since heated bedding is not meant for young children. It also does nothing for the dry, scratchy air that heating leaves behind; for that, see our guide to the <a href="/best-humidifier-for-bedroom/">best humidifier for a bedroom</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Electric Blanket, Heated Throw or Heated Mattress Pad: Pick by Where You Get Cold
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric blankets, heated throws and heated mattress pads all use the same idea, thin insulated heating wires inside fabric run by a controller, but they put the heat in different places. Choosing the right type matters more than choosing the brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric Blanket: Heat From Above</h3>

          <p>
            An electric blanket goes on the bed in place of, or under, your usual top blanket. It suits people who feel the cold on top, couples who want separate controls in a queen or king, and anyone who likes the weight of a thick blanket. Queen and king sizes usually come with dual controllers, one for each side.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heated Throw: Heat Where You Sit</h3>

          <p>
            A heated throw is usually about 50 by 60 inches and is meant for a sofa, armchair or desk chair. It is the cheapest way to stop heating a whole living room so that one person can sit still in it. Throws generally have shorter auto-off timers, because they are often left on when someone dozes off in front of the television.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heated Mattress Pad: Heat From Below</h3>

          <p>
            A heated mattress pad fits over the mattress under the fitted sheet, so you keep your own duvet. It suits people who get cold through the mattress, and it is the type to look at if you want the bed warm before you get in. Check the pocket depth against your mattress, plus any topper, so it stays flat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">12V Blankets: Heat From a Battery</h3>

          <p>
            A 12-volt blanket plugs into a car socket or the matching port on a portable power station. It is gentler than a household blanket, but it is the one that keeps working in a power cut without an inverter.
          </p>

